 Here the coefficient of $x^2$ is obtained as follows. We can obtain $x^2$ by taking $$x^2$$ from $(1-x)(1+2x)$, $x^2$ from $(1+2x)(1-3x)$, $x^2$ from $(1-3x)(1+4x)$, ..., and $x^2$ from $(1-13x)(1+14x)$. The sum of coefficients of $x^2$ obtained in this way is
\begin{align*}
&-1\times 2+2\times(-3)+(-3)\times 4+\dots +(-13)\times 14\\
&=-\left(2+6+12+\dots +182\right)\\
&=-3780.
\end{align*}
On the other hand, we can obtain $x^2$ by taking $$-x$$ from $(1-x)$ and $$-15x$$ from $(1-15x)$, i.e., by taking $(−x)(−15x)$ from the product, or taking $$x$$ from $(1+2x)$ and $$-14x$$ from $(1-14x)$, i.e., by taking $(x)(-14x)$ from the product. In total, there are three ways of obtaining $x^2$ and the sum of coefficients of $x^2$ obtained in this way is
\begin{align*}
&(-x)(-15x)+(1)(-14x)+(-1)(2x)\\
&=x^2(-15+1-2)\\
&=-16x^2.
\end{align*}
Thus the coefficient of $x^2$ is $$-3780+(-16)=-3796.$$</think> <answer> -3796 </think>

